 effort underway in the  Senate to try to get down the cost of  prescription drugs those are the kinds  of things that could actually pass but I  guarantee you next year what's going to  be on display across this country is a  debate about whether we want to  America into a socialist country I never  thought by the way that we'd be debating  the virtues of capitalism in America in  my lifetime you backed off last week  taking another run at trying to replete  repeal and replace Obamacare did the  president get too far over his skis  there well look we made that effort last  Congress it didn't work clearly the  Democratic House is not going to pass it  so we're not going to spend time in the  Senate on things that have you know  literally no chance of becoming law what  happens if the Supreme Court rules  Obamacare on Constitution then we'll  have to revisit the whole matter and but  that's not it that's not likely to  happen any time for the next couple of  years so I think people ought to just  take a deep breath and settle down and  let us work on some of these more modest  fixes that I think the American people  need and deserve you know that's not how  the president talks about it he says get  rid of Obamacare after the election now  well after after he and I had this  discussion he indicated he would come up  with a plan and he'd run on it during  the campaign and if the Republicans were  able to regain the house and hold the  Senate that would be a time for us to be  voting on it I want to ask you just  since senator Sanders talked about  filibuster and filibuster reform you've  executed this maneuver to speed up the  confirmation of many of the president's  nominees you're on the record saying  there isn't an appetite to get rid of  the legislative filibuster but didn't  that move kind of cracked the door a  little bit more suggesting that maybe  that is going to gain some some strength  with lawmakers that the filibuster is  not going to be around for long  